<interrogationes unitas="polynomium">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I build a polynomial from text and print it back?
    <responsum symbolum="polynomium_ex_chorda polynomium_ad_chordam">polynomium_ex_chorda(chorda_ex_literis("3t^2
      - t + 1 - 2t^-1", piscina), 't', piscina, &amp;p) returns FALSUM on
      bad text and leaves p untouched. A term is an optional decimal
      coefficient, then optionally the letter with ^ and an exponent
      that may start with '-'; terms are joined by + or -, only the first
      may carry a leading '-' (a leading '+', "3*t" or "+ -3" is
      refused), spaces and tabs may sit between sign, coefficient and
      letter but not around ^ ("t ^2" is refused), terms come in any
      order and repeated exponents are added.
      The letter must be ASCII a-z or A-Z. polynomium_ad_chordam prints
      the canonical form, highest degree first, "0" for zero.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why do multiplica, potentia, translata and friends return b32 instead of the polynomial?
    <responsum symbolum="polynomium_multiplica polynomium_potentia polynomium_monomium" via="lib/polynomium.worklog.md">Exponents
      are bounded by POLYNOMIUM_EXPONENS_MAXIMUS, 2^30 - 1, so that every
      span fits i32 and every exponent product fits s64; with the full
      s32 range a sum's coefficient count wrapped to 0 and corrupted
      memory. Any operation that takes or moves an exponent returns
      FALSUM instead of overflowing, and leaves *exitus untouched.
      nega, adde, subtrahe, multiplica_scalari and inversum cannot leave
      the range and return the value directly.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I keep a polynomial after rolling back or destroying the piscina its inputs came from?
    <responsum symbolum="polynomium_transcribe polynomium_translata magnus_transcribe">Only
      after polynomium_transcribe. Results may share coefficient values
      (and their magnus limbs) with the arguments, and
      polynomium_translata shares the whole coefficient array without
      allocating, so a result can point into the inputs' piscina.
      polynomium_transcribe copies the array and every coefficient
      through magnus_transcribe into the piscina you name.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the difference between polynomium_translata and polynomium_translatum?
    <responsum symbolum="polynomium_translata polynomium_translatum">polynomium_translata(p,
      k) is p times t^k: it only moves the exponents (shares the
      coefficients, refuses if out of range). polynomium_translatum(p, c)
      is the Taylor shift p(t + c) with a magnus c: the coefficients
      change, and it refuses any negative exponent.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I store a sparse polynomial like t^1000000 + 1 cheaply?
    <responsum symbolum="polynomium_ex_chorda polynomium_ex_coefficientibus" via="lib/polynomium.worklog.md">No:
      the representation is dense, summus - imus + 1 coefficients, so
      t^1000000 alone is one coefficient but t^1000000 + 1 is 1000001.
      polynomium_ex_chorda refuses a span above
      POLYNOMIUM_AMPLITUDO_LECTIONIS (2^20) because a 30-byte text could
      otherwise ask for gigabytes; the arithmetic itself has no such
      limit, so a wide adde or multiplica simply allocates.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I turn a Kauffman bracket in A into a Jones polynomial in t?
    <responsum symbolum="polynomium_monomium polynomium_multiplica polynomium_contrahe polynomium_ex_chorda" via="lib/polynomium.worklog.md">With
      writhe w, the factor (-A^3)^(-w) is the single monomial
      (-1)^w A^(-3w): build it with polynomium_monomium and multiply the
      bracket by it with polynomium_multiplica, then call
      polynomium_contrahe(f, -IV, piscina, &amp;jones), since A =
      t^(-1/4). contrahe returns FALSUM if some exponent is not
      divisible by 4. The tests turn the right trefoil's -A^5 - A^-3 +
      A^-7 (w = 3, factor -A^-9) into -t^4 + t^3 + t this way.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I evaluate a polynomial at a number?
    <responsum symbolum="polynomium_valor fractio_ex_s64">polynomium_valor(p,
      x, piscina, &amp;exitus) with x a Fractio (fractio_ex_s64 for an
      integer): Horner over exact rationals, then times x^imus. It
      returns FALSUM only when x = 0 and p has a negative
      exponent.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I divide one polynomial by another?
    <responsum symbolum="polynomium_divide_exacte polynomium_divisor_communis">polynomium_divide_exacte
      gives the quotient when b divides a exactly in Z[t, t^-1] (t is a
      unit there) and FALSUM otherwise, or when b is zero. There is no
      division with remainder or pseudo-division in the public API;
      polynomium_divisor_communis computes a gcd internally with
      pseudo-remainders.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I take the gcd or square-free part of a Laurent polynomial?
    <responsum symbolum="polynomium_divisor_communis polynomium_pars_libera polynomium_translata">No:
      polynomium_divisor_communis and polynomium_pars_libera accept only
      ordinary polynomials (no negative exponent) and return FALSUM
      otherwise, because in Z[t, t^-1] a gcd is only defined up to a
      unit t^k. Shift first with polynomium_translata. The gcd is over
      Q[t], returned primitive with a positive leading coefficient;
      gcd(0, 0) is 0.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the canonical form, and how do I tell the zero polynomial apart?
    <responsum symbolum="polynomium_est_nullum polynomium_gradus_imus polynomium_gradus_summus polynomium_aequalis polynomium_normale polynomium_est_symmetricum">Zero
      has no coefficients and gradus 0, the same degrees as the constant
      1, so test polynomium_est_nullum. Every other result has non-zero
      lowest and highest coefficients, which is why polynomium_aequalis
      can compare parts. polynomium_normale shifts to imus 0 with a
      positive lowest coefficient (Alexander's form);
      polynomium_est_symmetricum asks whether the coefficients read the
      same reversed with the SAME sign. The struct members are private:
      use the accessors.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does polynomium_apex_officinarum measure?
    <responsum symbolum="polynomium_apex_officinarum polynomium_multiplica polynomium_divide_exacte" via="lib/polynomium.worklog.md">The
      peak bytes used in the internal scratch piscinae (officinae) by
      the most recent multiplica or divide_exacte call, including the
      ones potentia, divisor_communis and pars_libera make internally:
      every call resets it, so it is 0 when that call ran in the
      caller's piscina. Officinae are used only when the term-by-term
      work is at least LXIV products and some partial sum could leave
      s64, so the caller keeps only the result. It exists so tests can
      assert scratch is rolled back.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
